{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SRjrWKUG7BAUeBo7xnrQjz","type":"tool_result","content":"## Scanners\n\nA scanner is a directory, `.ard/scanners/<name>/`, holding either an executable `scan` that prints findings as JSON lines or a `prompt.txt` for an LLM scanner. The controller does all dedupe, grouping, filing and closing, so deterministic tools and LLM scanners share one pipeline and no scanner hold\n\n```\n{\"fingerprint\":\"osv:GHSA-xxxx:go.mod:golang.org/x/net\",\"title\":\"golang.org/x/net: GHSA-xxxx (high)\",\n \"severity\":\"high\",\"group\":\"go.mod\",\"files\":[\"go.mod\"],\"body\":\"markdown details\"}\n```\n\n**The output schema is strict.** Each line is one JSON object with exactly these keys; blank lines are skipped. `fingerprint` (required, 1 to 512 bytes of valid UTF-8, no control characters), `title` (required, one line, at most 200 characters), `severity` (required: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `l\n\n**Each run, in order:**\n\n1. **Reconcile.** Fetch the scanner's open issues, plus a lookup of each issue that has a tracked finding and is no longer open, and update its state. Every marker line on an open issue becomes a row on that issue (a fingerprint on two open issues goes to the lowest number), so a crash between creat\n2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included: a scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed is\n3. **Scan.** Run the scanner on a read-only worktree of `origin/main`, with a timeout. It needs a free job slot, and scanners rank last for one. **A run that is denied a slot is not dropped: it is not recorded, stays due and is offered again on the next tick** (every five minutes), until it gets a s\n4. **Diff.** A new, unsuppressed fingerprint is queued for filing. A suppressed fingerprint stays quiet unless its severity rises above the suppressed one. A fingerprint that returns after being fixed files a new issue that links the old one as a regression. An open issue whose fingerprints are all \n5. **Group.** Findings that share a `group` key join one issue, up to `group_size` (N), as a checklist with one line per finding (ticked lines count towards N). An empty `group` means one issue per finding. New findings join an open group issue of the same group that has room and is not yet claimed,\n6. **File.** Edits and closes first, then joins, then new issues. Create the issue with `ard:scanner:<name>` and `ard:pending`, then queue `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` to the committer. The issue comes first because its number names the directory. A committer failure is logged and does not undo the issu\n\n**A scanner loop runs at most one due scanner per tick.** The `scanners` loop polls every five minutes, picks the most overdue scanner (last run plus its `every`; a dropped run counts as a run) and runs it. Pausing stops it, because it files and closes issues. Its tick timeout is the longest scan ti\n\n**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub is the truth.** Every scanner keeps a separate SQLite file, `~/.ard/state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`: fingerprint to issue, first and last seen, suppressions, last run. Scanners never share or lock each other's state, and one can be reset without touchi\n\n`ard rebuild-state <scanner>` takes the leader lock (it refuses to run while `ard serve` holds it), reads every issue with `ard:scanner:<name>`, open and closed, and replaces the scanner's findings and suppressions in one transaction, keeping the run history. For each fingerprint, an open issue wins\n\n**Dry-run files nothing.** Under `--dry-run` a would-be issue gets a synthetic negative number, stored in the scanner's dry-run database like a real one, so repeated scans do not re-propose it and `max_open` is reached from synthetic issues. The committer is never called for a synthetic number. The \n\n**Closed is not always \"forget\".** Removing a fingerprint on every close, as first sketched, refiles a won't-fix issue on the next run. Completed closes stop tracking the finding but keep a `fixed` tombstone, so a finding that comes back is filed as a regression of the old issue. Not-planned closes,\n\n**LLM scanners need stable fingerprints.** Their prose changes run to run, so they fingerprint on rule, file and symbol, never on wording. Before filing, the controller also compares the title against the scanner's open issues and skips near-duplicates.\n\n**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at \n\n| Scanner | Engine | Every | Group size | Auto-close |\n1:module github.com/albertcbrown/ard\n3:go 1.27\n# Pinned image for the docs scanner: just the Go toolchain, which builds the\n# stdlib-only docscan.go at scan time. The base image (golang:1.27.4-trixie,\n# git 2.47) is the same as the cve scanner's, pinned by multi-arch index digest,\n# verified 2026-11-23 with:\n#   docker buildx imagetools inspect golang:1.27.4-trixie\n# Docker ignores the tag when a digest is present; it is there for readers.\n#\n# The image does NOT copy `scan` or `docscan.go`: the executor mounts this role\n# directory read-only, so the mounted files are always the ones that run.\n# Run:  docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/repo:ro -v <role dir>:/role:ro -w /repo IMAGE /role/scan\n\nFROM golang:1.27.4-trixie@sha256:f18c3d73a184fb0582828510722e723b63a93f7188d11dd223d9be6f6880e65d\n# The executor's /tmp is a noexec tmpfs and go build executes from its temp dir,\n# so TMPDIR is /var/tmp: exec-capable and writable by any --user.\nENV GOTOOLCHAIN=local\nENV TMPDIR=/var/tmp\n127:def safeid: gsub(\"[^A-Za-z0-9._:-]\"; \"